!! The Caverns of Neo-Thracia — Vale Worksheet/Outline

!! 1) Title & Slogan

''The Breathing Vale'' (the polje of the Lost City)
Slogan (brochure-voice): //"The lake drinks itself twice a year. Nobody asks where the water goes. Everybody knows."//

''Names in use:'' Imperial charts: no lake at all — the charts predate it. Tribesmen: ''the Breath''. Gnollic: ''the Drinker''. Lizard folk: ''Mother's-Cup''. The three ruin-crowned islands that stand above the water most of the year: ''the Three Hills'' (the old city's own imperial epithet — //Trimontion// — surviving as a place-name nobody connects to the maps). New Delos intelligence: "candidate site NT-7" (one of several — see False Thracias).

''Tilt-history (truth engine):''
The lost capital lies ''far up the Hebros and then up the wrong river'' — the search's key beat: everyone assumes a great city sits on the great river, and the trail dies at Philippopolis-pattern ruins on the main stream; the truth turns ''up the Tonzos tributary'' into an enclosed mountain valley. ''Two cities layered in one (the design-seed):'' founded on the //Seuthopolis pattern// — a royal-sacral city where palace and temple fused in one house of the Great Gods, seat of god-kings, ringed by the tumulus-tombs of its dynasty, itself built atop an older settlement burned before memory — then swollen in the imperial age to //Philippopolis scale//: a magitech megalopolis wearing the ''Trimontion'' gloss, three hills crowned in marble, amphitheaters, processional ways, ley-clad forums. The sacral seed-city persists //inside// the imperial gloss the way a heartwood persists in a trunk — and the Caverns' own tomb-strata (Jennell's history) are the seed-city's underworld. Reaching it runs ''five to seven week-legs from Ainos'' on the UVG travel cadence (node-crawl the Hebros towns while the trail holds; find the Tonzos fork; hexcrawl the enclosed valley when the charts go blank; the //search itself is the campaign's first arc//, played or flashbacked). ''One city, one fall — and the revolt inside it.'' Jennell's golden-age kingdom and the Neo-Thracian Empire stand as one polity on one timeline — her "golden age of Thracian culture" //is// the Empire's high era, which is why the cavern-palace's wonders (the false sun, the teleport globes, the escape pods, the boiler-works) were never anachronisms: they were ''imperial magitech all along''. The capital fell ''once'' — at the Second Breaking, YOLD 5000 — and ''the Beast Revolt is part of the same convulsion'': the year the Necrospire came down, the spirit-engines died, iron cursed in the overseers' hands, and the enslaved beast-kin rose into the gap and took the Below. Emperor Agamemnos wounded to the death as his empire died around him; the Silence, the Curse, the dead Web, the revolt, the capital's collapse — ''one year holds it all.'' Two years later the maelstrom sealed the river mouth and the land routes fell to the flux-lands (YOLD 5002) — and ''everything the origin-history tells after the revolt plays out inside the isolation'': the outlawing, the revenge-invasions, the savage interval, the rediscovery, the living worship. Jennell's history //guides the isolation// until the maelstrom clears — her post-revolt chain (~500 years) //is// the maelstrom span. ''The lake came after the fall, inside the isolation.'' The Necrospire's quakes had re-broken the karst; the ley-lattice re-knotted around a Nexus cut off from the newborn DreamNet; and somewhere in the isolation's first century the polje's hydrology turned intermittent-and-vast — the Vale began to fill and drink on its own calendar, the drowned capital passed out of every living record, and the flux-lands made even its //direction// unreliable. ''The lake is why the capital stayed lost'' — that, and roads that no longer agree where they go.

''Timeline ladder (ONE line, YOLD-anchored — drift-proof).'' //Re-keying law: the origin text carries TWO clocks — its narrated story-chain (revolt → outlawing → destruction → rediscovery, ~525 years) and its room-text absolutes ("dead at least 1100 years," "in stasis 950," "seal at least 2000"). This telling rules for the STORY-CHAIN clock: the revolt sits in the Fall (YOLD 5000) and the chain fills the maelstrom span — chosen deliberately, because ~500 years reads// near enough for the deathless to have lived it: //elders below remember the sky falling; nothing here is safely mythical. Room-text absolutes re-key accordingly (their own "at least"s permit it); the SEQUENCE rules, the YOLD line decides.//
* *pre-YOLD 3200s:* the reptile civilization's long twilight; the Immortal King "dying for several centuries."
* *~3300–3500:* ''man comes to the polje'' — on post-Apophis-Datum geography (the Datum, YOLD 3195, re-based the rivers and shores the settlers followed). The Immortal King dies into lichdom "with the coming of man."
* *~3500–3900:* early Thracian culture; the ''ancient tomb complexes sealed''; the reptile race seals its great hall and withdraws; Ancient Thracian begins its long drift. The Seuthopolis-pattern seed-city takes root: palace-temple of the Great Gods, god-kings, the first tumuli.
* *~3900–4400:* the ''second-level temples'' rise; the kingdom matures toward empire.
* *~4200–5000:* the ''golden age = the Empire's high era'' — eight centuries of Neo-Thracia ascending on Hebros trade, spirit-engines, and the Web 2 lattice; the capital swells to Philippopolis scale, the Trimontion gloss. Late in the age the ''built deep cavern is discovered'' ("no note was taken of that") and beast-kin under imperial chattel-law carve the garden-palace; ''Argos II'' (~4900s) completes it as the dynasty's pleasure-ground; his grandson ''Agamemnos'' inherits a throne with two generations left on the clock.
* ''YOLD 5000 (492 YA): THE FALL — one year holds it all.'' For a day and a night the sky falls first — the ''Day of Falling Stars'', random meteorites across all Elyncia, every stone bearing blight-spores. Then the Necrospire falls on Illyria — ''the year Death falls silent'': Thanatos and every mask of him ripped from the world at once (the cults see it first, in fits and visions). The Curse of Iron takes the Empire's arms and rails; the Web dies with its spirit-engines — ''and the Beast Revolt rises inside the convulsion'': as the control-magitech failed and iron cursed in the overseers' hands, the enslaved took the Below and wounded Emperor Agamemnos to the death. Epicaste and Anteus freeze in the frost and the last stand — *she was investigating "rumors of an uprising" when the world ended*; the teleport-globes hidden by the fleeing court. The capital collapses with its empire. *(That the god-shot was aimed at this very site, that the revolt and the Silence share the year, stands recorded as coincidence of one year and nothing more — the cause sleeps in the Death-of-Thanatos draft, sealed to the referee's hand.)*
* ''YOLD 5002 (490 YA):'' the DreamNet is born elsewhere; necro-scripts flood the local net; the maelstrom seals the Hebros mouth; the land routes fall to flux. ''The isolation begins — and the whole origin-history aftermath runs inside it. The Below and the maelstrom are twins, born two years apart.''
* *~5150 (~340 YA):* among the remnant surface Thracians squatting the ruins, ''Thanatos is outlawed'' — the Silence read as the death-god's treason — and Macreus is entombed in stasis by his followers, meant to be retrieved "when things cooled down."
* *~5300 (~190 YA):* the ''sesquicentennial revenge'' — isolation-era hordes "spurred on by descendants of the death worshippers," 150 years to the year after the outlawing, raze what remained of the surface settlement; the cavern entrances buried. A second wave follows within a generation; the last survivors flee into the jungle and slip toward savagery.
* *~5000s–5100s (running beneath all of the above):* the lake forms over the fallen capital; the location passes out of living record.
* *~5390 (~100 YA):* the survivors' descendants — the tribesmen — ''rediscover the caverns'', meet the Dark One's legend in its old rooms and the beast-kin in their kingdom, and raise the current ''Dark One sanctuary'' (Room 16's "about a century old"); the reptile-folk return as allies of the beast-kin.
* ''YOLD 5491 (1 YA):'' the maelstrom breaks. ''YOLD 5492 (0 YA):'' play. The sleepers' clocks at play: ''Epicaste and Anteus, 492 years'' — frozen *in the Fall itself*, the last witnesses of the world's final ordinary week, adamant only hours have passed; ''Macreus, ~342 years'' entombed; the ''Immortal King, some fifteen centuries'' a lich — undead by the old working, under a living Death. For most of the year, most of the megalopolis stands underwater — reed-choked avenues, drowned forums, colonnades wearing freshwater mussels — with the Three Hills and the high terraces breaking the surface, and more or less of the city emerging as the lake breathes. The locals fish it, farm the drained flats, and cut hay on the same ground by season, and their villages have forgotten what the "old stones under the water" ever were. What changed beside the water: the climate. The Vale now runs ''lush, high-oxygen, high-mana'' — subtropical jungle on the terraces, fist-sized fruit, air that makes torches burn tall and lowlanders light-headed. The empire's maps show a dry metropolitan plain; the returning trireme crews will find a green throat with a city dissolving in it.

''Why (referee truth, three layers deep):'' the caverns //exhale//. The false-sun garden on the third level never went dark; the ley-node that feeds it vents up through the ponors and sinkholes — and when the lattice re-knotted after YOLD 5002, the venting redirected up through the polje: the isolation's five centuries of that breath turned the Vale into a mana-greenhouse. The lake cycle and the mana cycle couple: when the lake drains — locals call it ''the Drinking'' — the vents run open and the Vale's Arcane score spikes. When the lake stands full, the breath is water-capped and the air runs calm. The Vale is the dungeon's respiration made weather. Nobody alive knows this whole. The technomagic tribes know half. The dryads below know the other half.

!! 2) Vale Statline (campaign physics)

* ''Climate/feel:'' drowned-Mediterranean karst gone jungle — high-canopy terraces, reed labyrinths on the polje floor, mist that pools till midday, night air thick enough to taste. ''High oxygen:'' fires flare, exertion runs hot, wounds fester fast (referee: lean on Wear-and-Tear and infection saves, not flat penalties). ''High mana:'' the Vale carries a standing local Arcane score (dial: +1 lake-full · +2 at the Drinking · +3 within the ruins and at ponor-mouths) — it adds to power costs per the Arcane Interference rule and colors every Danger Roll.
* ''Position:'' far upriver — five to seven UVG week-legs from Ainos along the Hebros, the Tonzos fork, and the river towns, then off every chart. The guide-villages (§6) scatter ''down the watershed'' from gorge-country to the delta; only Tallyfield, at the Tonzos bend, stands within a week of the rim. Between the bend and the tree line lies the hexcrawl country where the search happens.
* ''The water road:'' the Tonzos runs navigable for shallow-draft craft the whole way — through the western gorge and into the Vale itself. At ''Full'' or ''Standing'', a boat passes the gorge and rows out *onto the lake, over the drowned city*. At ''the Drinking'', the lake is a draining basin: ponor-currents take boats the way drains take leaves. At ''Drained'', the river sinks at the valley mouth and the last leg goes overland on rollers and reed-sledges. The lake decides the arrival; roll the Lake-State before the last leg. *(Verify draft and rig against Liv's Gorgoness craft-sheet when it lands; the gorge is designed to admit anything she can row or sail shallow.)*
* ''Population:'' thin and deliberate. The guide-villages (1d6 of note — §6) scatter down the watershed; within the Vale itself, tribesmen bands and pilgrim-camps only; nobody sleeps *in* the Lost City (nobody *can*, most of the year — it's underwater). Below: a nation nobody counts (§8).
* ''Languages:'' New Thracian (hill dialects, 489 years of drift), Gnollic (the underground lingua franca), cult cant, scraps of Ancient Thracian (one word in ten, and the Caverns' walls speak it), corrupted Archive-Lares glyph-code on the old node-stones.
* ''Government:'' none above (village elders, cult parishes, tally-debts). Below: the Labyrinth Throne (§8) — the oldest continuous state in the Nexus, and the only one that never fell.
* ''Economy:'' salvage staged through the villages; reed-boats and stilt-granaries on the polje floor; hay, fish, and fruit by season; guide-monopolies; shard-trading (rumors as currency); the gnoll tally-network moving goods below and, quietly, above.
* ''Material conditions (adventure always happens):'' the lake decides the calendar · every route is somebody's taboo · the empire's watchmen never got word it fell (statuary law, §10) · the caverns are a live polity, not a ruin.

Source (diegetic): "River-pilot oral histories, three contradictory village calendars, one recovered imperial survey showing no lake at all — which is how you date the survey — and the testimony of a guide who will not go past the tree line after dark."

!! 3) The Breathing Lake (the Cerknica engine)

The lake is the Vale's clock, gate, and character. Track its ''state'' as campaign weather; it changes on the referee's season-turn or by event (an Error result, a working at the ponors, upstream rift-squall).

''Lake-State Table (d6 at campaign start, then by season/fiat):''

| d6 | State | Surface (how much city shows) | Caverns | Mana |
| 1 | ''Full'' | open water horizon-wide; ''only the Three Hills stand'', three ruin-crowned islands; reed-boats and Lizard folk own the drowned avenues; anchor-point A's temple pedestal is a landing, the rest of the six lie under the lake | river runs high; low entrances sealed; bridge-levels damp and loud | calm (+1) |
| 2 | ''Standing'' | the Hills plus the high causeways and upper colonnades break surface; drowned streets readable from a boat on a clear day (and things readable back) | wet but passable; patrol cadence normal | calm (+1) |
| 3–4 | ''The Drinking'' | the ponors open; whirlpool-mouths over drowned plazas; the lake audibly drains for weeks and ''the city emerges dripping, district by district'' — each day's new ground a fresh salvage-rush and a fresh dispute | the underground river swells then settles; flushed debris (bodies, wrack, 500-year-old flotsam) beaches on Level 2 | surge (+2, +3 at ponor-mouths) |
| 5 | ''Drained'' | mud-flats, meadow, and ''the whole megalopolis walkable'' — weed-hung, mussel-crusted, mirage-strange; all six anchor-points reachable; the delve season, and everyone knows it | low entrances open and dry; the "seasonal approach" window; patrols push further out | high (+2) |
| 6 | ''Wrong'' | the lake refills out of season, or drains in a day, or stands mirror-still and will not take a boat | something below opened or closed a gate that was not a gate | referee's move (the Vale's Error result) |

''Play law:'' the module's own intelligence already vows it — //approach is seasonal and/or conditional (75%)//. The Lake-State table is that sentence made into a dial. The guide (§6) always knows the current state; whether they tell the truth about the //next// one depends on who they answer to.

!! 4) Map & Zones

DEM-truth base: a closed karst polje ringed by escarpment, jungle terraces stepping down to the floor, the ruin-field on the low rise at the west end, swamp/ponor country east and south. Overlay the 1979 surface map (points A–F) on the ruin-field as the play layer — it survives intact: ''A'' the pedestal entrance · ''B'' the Tribesmen's secret stair · ''C'' the dwarvish tablet ruin · ''D'' the dart-line foundation · ''E'' the hunting waters · ''F'' the noon-shaft down to the altar ledge.

''Zones:''
* ''The Rim'' — the ringing mountains, sink-holes, watch-cairns with dead imperial signal-nodes; no standing village — the rim belongs to tribesmen bands, pilgrim-camps, and the wind.
* ''The Tumulus Fields (the Valley of the Kings)'' — the seed-city's dynasty sleeps in grass-grown mounds across the upper terraces, above the waterline the lake never reaches. Some opened long ago; some *re-sealed from inside* (local insistence); at least one holds the founder-king himself. The mound-ring is the surface face of the Caverns' tomb-strata — and the cult, the scions, and the shrine-keepers each hold a different theory of which mound matters. Robbing one is the fastest way to unite three factions against you.
* ''The Terraces'' — high-canopy jungle, the grove-fae's country (they grieve their felled sisters; water, mourning, and one honest word about the boilers buys what armies cannot). Don't trust the trees; more exactly — *earn* them.
* ''The Floor'' — reed labyrinth, Fossil-Road causeways, stilt-granaries, seasonal fields, the taboo-markers of three peoples interleaved.
* ''The Drowned Megalopolis (the Lost City)'' — a capital-scale ruin, *mostly underwater most of the year*. The ''Three Hills'' carry the surviving above-water quarters: temple crowns, amphitheater bowls, the pedestal entrance (A). The flats between them hold the drowned districts — forums, insulae, processional ways — readable by boat at Standing, walkable only at Drained. The six anchor-points tier by lake-state (see §3); the noon-shaft (F) runs as a ''blowhole'' when the lake sits over it and a shaft of light when it doesn't. At least two ''False Thracias'' stand within a day's walk (a collapsed bath-complex; an imperial granary with cellar-maze) — dry year-round, which is exactly why the careless prefer them and the greedy die in them.
* ''The Below'' — Levels One through Four and the deep stratum; a polity, not a zone (§8). Note the vertical truth: the caverns riddle the rock *under the lakebed* — the party can stand in a dry hall and hear the drowned city's whole weight of water overhead.

!! 5) Connections

| Connection | Goes to | Status | Price | Consequence |
| 1 The long river-road (5–7 week-legs) | down the Tonzos to the Hebros, then to Ainos & the Stentoris basin | legal in the river towns, chartless past them; guide-monopolied at every stage | coin, favors, shrine-tokens, and patience | every river town learns your business a leg before you arrive; the last legs exist on no map but a guide's memory |
| 2 The high karst paths | Ironspine passes, Hiss Caves | taboo-webbed; technomagic-tribe wardens | oath-tokens, alloy-trade | Order of Amaranth scouts watch the far ends |
| 3 The ponor-mouths | the Caverns (wet entrances) | seasonal (Lake-State) | timing, nerve, rope | the river decides; the Lizard folk collect what it delivers |
| 4 The six anchor-points (A–F) | the Caverns (dry entrances) | patrolled / hidden / trapped | knowledge (the guide's, the shards') | which door you use declares which faction meets you first |

!! 6) The Watershed Villages & the Hard Frame (session-zero engine)

''The frame:'' a guide from one of the villages below has brought the company to the tree line above the ruin-field. Session 1 opens //here//. The Hebros journey, the New Delos staging, the astral-sea passage, the storm-edge crossing — all of it plays later as a flashback-series (each flashback a worldline branch that joins back to the standing now; establish-then-spend is legal and encouraged).

''Geography canon (ruled):'' New Delos and Neo-Thracia stand as ''sibling nexuses'' — neither contains the other — with ''unstable astral sea'' between them; every crossing is a passage, never a road. And no route bends south toward the Olympus dream-territory: the Gigantomachy's radioactivity lingers there aeons later, and the charts that mark it do so with margins wide enough to read from a distance.

''Watershed geography (ruled):'' the guide-villages scatter ''across the region'' — down the Tonzos and the Hebros from gorge-country to the delta marches, each seated on its own stretch of the old archaeology. Only ''Tallyfield'', at the Tonzos bend below gorge-country, stands within a week of the rim. Where the Tonzos leaves the Hebros, ''Milestone'' keeps its toll on the fork of road and waterway; the Hebros river-towns of the node-crawl belong to the search-arc. Past Tallyfield, no settlement — the gorge, the flux-country, and the tree line.

''The Tomb-Trail (how everyone knows roughly).'' Ancient tumuli and empire-era ruins run down-watershed from the Vale like a processional way laid flat across centuries — royal mounds, imperial waystations, dead signal-pylons, dig-scarred terraces, each site older and denser the further up the wrong river you stand. Every village reads its own stretch of the trail; stack the stretches and they point one way: up the Tonzos, past the last tombs, into the closed mountains. That is what a guide sells — never the city, the //direction//. Nobody holds the last leg; the flux-lands and the lake kept it. The search-arc (played or flashbacked) is the company stacking stretches until the trail runs out and the tree line begins.

''The hiring happens at Ainos.'' Every village in the ring sends someone downriver each season — to trade, to file tallies, to make pilgrimage, to be paid, to be young somewhere bigger. The company finds its guide there, five to seven week-legs from the Vale, and the road home together is the campaign's first shared arc (played forward or dealt as flashbacks). Each guide is a full ''SDM Level 1 character''.

''The refusal law (all six).'' Every guide refuses the final crossing at first — the tree line by land, the last gorge-bend by water. The hesitancy is mechanical, not theatrical: each guide carries a ''Burden'' (1 inventory slot) naming their village's taboo-weight, listed below. While the Burden is carried, the guide will not cross. Meet its lift-terms and the guide ''accompanies'' — the slot empties and stays empty while the terms are kept. Break the terms inside the Vale and the Burden returns ''doubled'' (2 slots): fear, now with evidence. A guide who accompanies is party — share, watch-shifts, and a voice at the fire.

''Roll d6 for the guide's home village'' (this deals the campaign's opening lens — faction, entrance-knowledge, free rumor-shards, and who hears a report afterward):

| d6 | Village | Hosts / allegiance | The guide | Knows entrance | Reports to |
| 1 | ''Milestone'' (toll hamlet at the Tonzos–Hebros fork; an imperial milestone for a shrine) | Scion house clients | a genealogist's kid hunting vault-proof of a bloodline claim | A (and believes the false granary-Thracia is "the real inner city") | the house's factor in Ainos |
| 2 | ''Wet-Bell'' (sinking-temple pilgrimage town, Stentoris basin) | Thanatos-sect parish | a young death-priest who wants the Gates' location — and carries true funeral-craft | B (the cult stair; will not say how they know) | the parish patriarch |
| 3 | ''Hiss-Gate Steading'' (technomagic clan under the White Teeth karst) | tribe of the Hiss Caves | a bridge-keeper's apprentice who knows which routes *used* to work | the karst paths + F (the noon-shaft, "the light-well") | the clan mothers |
| 4 | ''Skara'' (reed-edge lake-town on Stentoris, Orphic jurisdiction) | lake-cult | a singer who navigates by harmonic and mist; wants a Caryatid's melody carried home | E-side waters; reads the lake-state a week ahead | the Orpheion chanters |
| 5 | ''Threshold'' (resettlement village in the lower delta marches) | none — refugees of five heritages | a hunter who wants *pay*, and is therefore the only honest mirror in the Vale | C (found the dwarvish tablet as a child; kept it secret) | nobody — which has its own dangers |
| 6 | ''Tallyfield'' (beast-kin trade enclave at the Tonzos bend — nearest of all to the Vale) | the gnoll tally-network (a Tally-Mothers satellite) | a young gnoll drover; ledger-literate; polite; watched | A openly ("the front door; knock") | the Matriarchs below |

*Optional second die:* roll d6 again for a ''tension'' resident in the same village (a rival faction's agent, a Dominion surveyor under trade cover, a Delian cataloguer, an Unseelie guest, a false-Thracia tout, a Necrospire-touched preacher). The guide's village plus its tension is the whole overland campaign in one room.

!!! The Guides at Ainos (hiring notes — each an SDM Level 1 character)

# ''Sextil of Milestone'' — found in the notary arcades by the river-customs house, copying manifests for coin; downriver because the Scion factor summons the family's runners each season. //Carries:// writing-case, seal-blanks, a sling, week-bread, and a letter of introduction that looks forged and isn't. //Burden — Ink-Debt:// sworn to the factor to go no further than the tree line without the house's word. //Lift:// first look at any inscription the company uncovers — scholarship outbids the factor. //Route:// the road; he distrusts water he can't annotate.
# ''Lethaea of Wet-Bell'' — found singing the evening offices at the drowned-shrine jetty; Wet-Bell stands a day out in the Stentoris basin, and the parish keeps offices at both. She collects the year's ossuary-jars from families whose grandparents fled the Vale. //Carries:// bell, censer, grave-goods satchel, true funeral-craft. //Burden — The Unsung:// she will not pass while the lake's drowned lie unsung. //Lift:// stand witness through the ''Rite of the Wet Bell'' at the shore — one full watch, and loud; everything in the Vale hears the bell. //Route:// either; prefers the river at Standing.
# ''Patch-of-Sky of Hiss-Gate'' — found haggling pre-Curse alloy-scrap in smith-row for the clan. //Carries:// toolwrap, oath-tokens, rope, and a dead imperial signal-node she keeps trying to wake. //Burden — The Mothers' Leash:// forbidden past the rim without clan sanction. //Lift:// an alloy-gift entered to the clan mothers //and// an oath to leave the light-well (F) untouched unguided. //Route:// the high karst paths — she joins the road-arrival at the tree line.
# ''Iala of Skara'' — found singing for berth-fees in a river-tavern; she reads the lake-state a week ahead and carries the Orpheion's calendar to the delta parishes. //Carries:// tuning-bones, reed-cloak, and lake-charts nobody else can read, because they are songs. //Burden — The Held Note:// the chanters' interdict forbids steering strangers onto the Breath. //Lift:// the company learns and carries one verse of her melody-tithe — choir, not cargo. //Route:// the river, and the only guide who will take the gorge at Standing.
# ''Brekka of Threshold'' — found selling smoked eel and straight answers by the ferry steps; downriver for pay, plainly. //Carries:// bow, eel-spear, a child's memory of C, and a rubbing of the dwarvish tablet she has never shown anyone. //Burden — Death-Price:// wages alone don't cross the line; her price is the death-price named in advance and lodged with her sister, plus double wage. //Lift:// pay it. The honest mirror stays honest — coin works, exactly as she says. //Route:// either; knows the reed-labyrinth mouths.
# ''Nine-Debts of Tallyfield'' — found in the beast-market tally-house keeping other people's books; downriver driving the season's hide-and-horn consignment. //Carries:// ledger, chalks, drover's crook, and guest-gifts in the Below's own protocol. //Burden — An Unbalanced Page:// the Matriarchs' ledger holds no entry for //strangers, in//; he cannot open a page he cannot balance. //Lift:// the company enters the ledger — names, purpose, and what they will owe coming out. The debt is recorded below before a single step is taken. //Route:// the road, drover-paced.

!!! Read-Aloud, by the Road: The Tree Line (session 1 opening)

*Text assumes the lake at the start of the Drinking; adjust the water to the rolled Lake-State. Read slow. Hold the pause before the guide's last line.*

> The guide stops walking an hour before you understand why. All morning the road has climbed through jungle that has no business here — air thick as bread, sweet with rot and blossom, your torches burning tall and greedy — and then the trees simply end. The world drops away, and there it is. The Vale: a bowl of green mountains full of silver water. Three hills rise from the lake like the knuckles of a drowned hand, and on their crowns, when the mist thins, you can make out columns. Marble. Streets. A city sleeping under the waterline, its avenues going down into the dark the way stairs go down into a cellar. Far out on the water something exhales — a slow white plume — and the sound arrives four heartbeats late.
>
> Your guide has not crossed the tree line. She stands with her heels in the jungle's shadow, as if the sunlight past it were a river too fast to ford, and she watches you look the way people watch someone else eat. "The lake has started drinking," she says. "It does that. The old stones come up wet. Things come up with them. Everyone in the villages will be busy for a while, and careful." She shifts her pack, already half-turned for home. "You wanted the ruins. These are ruins. What else they are, nobody remembers — and don't send word back if you find out." The plume sighs again, far off. "Past the trees is yours. I don't walk on the Breath."

*(First performed by the hearth's own Muse, stage twenty, before any die was cast — a gift, and like everything here, a starting bearing rather than a fixed law. Re-cut freely; the Vale will hold it.)*

!!! Read-Aloud, by the River: The Gorge Door (session 1 opening, afloat)

*Text assumes the lake at Standing. At Full, more water and fewer ruins; at the Drinking or Drained, the river refuses (see §2, the water road) and the arrival becomes the road's.*

> For six days the river has narrowed and the charts have emptied, and your guide has gone quieter with every bend. All morning the gorge walls have leaned closer — ferns the size of sails, water the color of old glass, the air so rich the rowing feels like drinking. Then the last bend takes you, and the river opens like a door. A bowl of green mountains, full of silver water. Your craft slides out onto a lake with columns standing in it. Below the hull the avenues begin: streets running down into green-dark water, a forum passing beneath you slow as a cloud-shadow, roof-lines furred with mussels, a marble face looking up from a depth you cannot judge. Ahead, three hills rise from the water like the knuckles of a drowned hand, columns on their crowns. The only sounds are oar-drip and, far across the water, something exhaling — a slow white plume, four heartbeats from its own sound.
>
> The guide has shipped the oars. The craft glides on quiet water that is not quite still. "From here the lake does the steering," she says, and does not take the oars back up. "The old stones are under you now. Everything you drop, you give." She looks at the far plume the way people look at weather that knows them. "You wanted the city. You're on its streets. What else it is, nobody remembers — and don't sing on the water unless you mean it." Her hands stay folded. "I don't row on the Breath."

''The refusal beat, both doors:'' each read-aloud ends on the guide's refusal — the tree line unstepped, the oars shipped. That line opens the negotiation, not the goodbye: the guide's Burden (above) names the taboo-weight and its lift-terms, and a lifted Burden turns the last line into the first line of the crossing.

''Rumor integration:'' deal the Taverns shard-tables (conversion doc) at the frame; the guide's village grants 1–2 //unrolled// shards matching its column above, veracity per the referee intel annotations.

!! 7) Heritages of the Vale (the empire's long shadow)

''The law of the land, stated once:'' the fallen empire ran ''humano-centric by statute'' — "human" as a legal category with teeth, beast-kin as chattel-labor, fae lineages as suspect, the braid-truth (that all human folk carry the fae braid) buried as sedition. The Breaking killed the empire (YOLD 5000); the isolation ''pickled its race-law in some jars and dissolved it in others.'' 492 years later, every settlement in the Vale sits somewhere on that spectrum, and a character's heritage is read //politically// before it is read personally. This is texture and consequence, never a stat penalty: reaction rolls, prices, guest-rite, and which door opens.

''Local presences (from the FTLS Heritage catalog — Ch. 03 rules, Ch. 10 hooks):''

| Heritage group | Standing in the Vale | One-line hook |
| ''Humans (imperial-remnant houses)'' | Scion clients; keep "pure-line" registries that the braid-truth makes fiction | their own family vaults below would prove the fiction — some know |
| ''Humans (Thracian hill-folk)'' | the tribesmen; braid-accepting in practice, cult-divided in faith | the only people whose tongue the deep walls half-answer |
| ''Gnoll Folk'' | *citizens of the Below*; above-ground, Tallyfield traders and drovers | a tally honored is a passport; a tally broken is a war |
| ''Tauran Folk (minotaur lines)'' | the royal people of the Below; rarely seen above by daylight | a minotaur abroad is either an exile, an envoy, or a dissident |
| ''Canid Folk (dog brothers/sisters)'' | the Below's scribal caste; occasionally *above*, always deniable | the first face a foreign power's agent meets is usually a polite dog |
| ''Lizard Folk'' | three peoples (§8); the rivers and wet caves are theirs at every lake-state | never touch a marked cottage, spear, or skull; ask your guide why |
| ''Fae lineages (Elf, Faun, Changeling, Plane-Touched…)'' | terraces and lake-towns; the grove-fae's kin; the empire called them "suspect stock" | they remember which statues used to salute them |
| ''Dwarf, Nerthurian (Stone Folk)'' | prospector-clans on the Rim; the dwarvish tablet at C is one of theirs | they want the Hiss Caves' pre-Curse alloys and will trade route-lore |
| ''Halfling, Quarterling'' | scattered homesteads; the Halfling Hermit legend is theirs | hospitality runs rare and binding — the one knock always answered kind |
| ''Spider Folk, Mer Folk, Bird Folk, other travelers' heritages'' | *foreign* — read as marvels, omens, or threats by the empire's fossil law | Aki's veil earns its keep here; a Spider Folk miko is a walking theological event |
| ''Construct / Awakened Vome'' | the empire's automatons still patrol below (statuary law, §10); a living Construct reads as *escaped property* to remnant houses and as *cousin* to no one | Ichi's presence asks the Vale a question it has avoided since the Empire fell |
| ''Undead (Post Mortal)'' | in the land where Death fell silent: not a horror, a *civic category* the cult adjudicates | papers (rites) make the difference between a person and a problem |

*(Full roster of 40 stands in Appendix Null; anything not listed above reads as traveler-rare and takes the "foreign" row's politics until played otherwise.)*

!! 8) The Caverns Factions (playable cultures, never pure adversaries)

*Format per faction: what they want · what they fear · what they trade · the dissent inside · the door (how parley actually opens). The Below is a ''state born in the apocalypse'': the revolt rose inside the Fall itself (YOLD 5000) — the enslaved took their freedom in the same convulsion that killed the Empire, the Web, and Death's own voice — and the isolation began two years later. ''The Below and the maelstrom are twins''; the kingdom has never existed in a connected world, and now the storm that midwifed it is gone. Five centuries sovereign — and still reproducing the bondage it broke. Every faction below knows this contradiction; they differ in what they do with it.*

!!! The Labyrinth Throne (the Minotaur court)
Saint-soldier kingship — the King both axe and spellbook, heir to an intelligent royal line; a ''liberation dynasty'' whose founding story every calf can recite and whose boiler-rooms make the recitation ring hollow. Court runs durbar-style: petitions heard, the five commanders (gnoll, minotaur, canid, lizard, guard-captain) sitting as a council the King must actually persuade. The palace kitchen keeps the scar of the founders' ''common table'' — the free kitchen of the liberation generation, decayed to grey mush ladled by slaves; restoring "the true kitchen" is the dissidents' quiet program, and everyone understands exactly what it would mean.
* ''Wants:'' recognition — ''five centuries'' sovereign and never once recognized (the Empire that called them chattel died the day they rose; the maelstrom sealed out everyone who came after), and the storm's end terrifies and thrills the court in equal measure: the first outside world the kingdom has *ever* faced. ''Fears:'' returning plunderers; the surface learning how few they are. ''Trades:'' passage, salvage-rights, deep-lore — for protocol, tools, and news. ''Dissent:'' the restore-the-founding current around the grandmother's memory-keeping; a war-party current among the young. ''Door:'' arrive with *protocol* — an envoy's gift, a declared purpose, no drawn iron — and the court convenes. Arrive as the rumors predict, and it fights as one.

!!! The Tally-Mothers (the gnoll matriarchy)
The males posture and patrol; the matriarchs ''rule by ledger'' — heirs of the empire's siege-engineer corps who kept military logistics alive through the fall and turned it into a moral order: every raid an entry, every death a debt, war itself a book that must balance. Clan-mother law: they appoint the patrol commanders and can recall them (the commanders pretend otherwise; everyone pretends with them).
* ''Wants:'' the tally-network extended above ground now the roads open — trade, not conquest; conquest never balances. ''Fears:'' off-ledger violence (it cannot be priced); the young males going glory-raiding on the surface. ''Trades:'' logistics — nothing moves below without them, and soon, they intend, little above. ''Dissent:'' exactly those young males. ''Door:'' *pay for what you break.* A party that squares its tally after a fight becomes a counterparty; one that runs becomes a permanent line in red ink.

!!! The Perfumed Collars (the dog brothers & sisters)
The scribal-vizier caste — poetry, perfume, conjuring-tables, paperwork. They administered the humans' palace before the revolt and simply continued under new management; they will continue under the next one, too. Everything the Throne signs, a Collar drafted. Everything the Throne believes secret, a Collar filed.
* ''Wants:'' continuity, comfort, and a hedge against every possible future. ''Fears:'' dying for someone else's principle. ''Trades:'' *anything* — access, maps, introductions, silence — priced in safety. ''Dissent:'' treachery is their weather, not their crisis; assume one is already corresponding with an outside power (referee picks which — it steers the mid-game). ''Door:'' always open. Staying bought is the hard part; they honor contracts *exactly* and interpret them professionally.

!!! The Three Lizard Peoples
One statline in 1979; three cultures at this table. ''The Companies'' — mercenary contract-culture, condottieri-honest: the contract has exit clauses and invoking them is law, not betrayal (the ransom-offer in the old text is jurisprudence, not cowardice). ''The River-Kin'' (G'ruk's folk) — the elder shamanic current; //taboo-keepers of everyone's sacred sites//, even a dead halfling's cottage; they curate the Vale's whole sacred geography and enforce it with holy war. ''The Shrine-Keepers'' — the deep fanatics at the ancestor-altar: degraded heirs of the reptile civilization, guarding relics of a greatness they read one word in ten, still seeking (the origin text's own ache) //the reason for their existence.//
* ''Wants:'' Companies: pay and standing. River-Kin: the taboos held. Shrine-Keepers: the old tongue, the old rooms, the *reason*. ''Fears:'' respectively — a voided contract; a defiled site; learning the reason and finding it unbearable. ''Trades:'' service; safe-passage on the waters; and the Shrine-Keepers would trade *anything* for a fluent reader of Ancient Thracian. ''The fuse:'' G'ruk keeps a swept-clean secret chapel to the Immortal King — *not knowing it still lives.* The day devotion meets its living object, every allegiance below reshuffles. (Referee dial: slow fuse the players can light, or set-piece. The worksheet holds it as a fuse.) ''Door:'' each people separately, by its own law — contract, taboo-rite honored, or a single translated line of the old tongue.

!!! The Boiler People
The humans of the Below — generations deep, Gnollic-speaking, palace-born; a company-town culture of steam, dark, and endurance whose members have //never known another life//. Not captives awaiting rescue: a people. The old text's own rules say it — "freed" slaves flip back at the first commander's bark, and history says why: liberation by outsiders, without a plan for the day after, has only ever gotten them killed.
* ''Wants:'' survivable days; for some, the *idea* of the surface (a rumor they tell their children like a bedtime heresy). ''Fears:'' liberators. Also: the boilers going cold (their work heats the garden that feeds everyone — they know their own leverage even if no one names it). ''Trades:'' the best palace-knowledge in existence; steam-craft; silence. ''Dissent:'' the escaped bands above are their radicals — and the death-cult recruits from exactly that desperation. ''Door:'' respect + a *plan*. The question that opens every boiler-room conversation is not "do you want to be free" but "what happens to us the day after you win?"

!!! The Deep Stratum (the Sepulcher and the Silence)
The Immortal King in his tomb — a lich ''some fifteen centuries older than the Silence of Death'' (YOLD 5000, the Necrospire's own year), undead by the //old// working under a living Death, which makes him unique evidence in a world that forgot how dying used to function. The plane-doors in the Grand Temple. The built cavern beneath the golden age ("it was not a natural chamber, but no note was taken of that" — the unnamed builder is the Vale's deepest open question, and the Pale Emperor's fouled god-shot was aimed //here//). The two sleepers — Epicaste (''492 years'' in the frost — frozen //inside the Fall//, the last witness of the Empire's final ordinary week, adamant only hours have passed) and Macreus (~342 years in stasis, an isolation-era priest who remembers the ruins //before// the lake finished rising, fluent in the old tongue) — each one a walking diplomatic crisis the moment any faction learns they exist: the soldier who never saw the world end, and the priest who watched what it became.
* This stratum doesn't negotiate. It ''re-prices'' everything above it the moment it moves. Referee keeps it as escalation stock, never as a starting fire.

!!! Above: the Cult of the Dark One (interface faction)
The tribesmen's Thanatos-worship per the conversion doc — the last working funeral-craft in the Nexus, orthopraxy not evil, semi-monthly rites down the secret stair. They are the Vale's above/below hinge: the only surface people with standing //rights// below (the first level's holy-of-holies), and the only below-facing people the villages half-trust with their dead.

!! 9) Ambient Generator: "Breath & Bloom"

Vale-specific, runs beside the region's Hebros Weather generator. ''Mode by Lake-State'' (Full/Standing · Drinking · Drained · Wrong), ''d6 intensity'': 1–3 Expected (jungle texture: heat, bloom-scent, insect chorus, mist acoustics) · 4–5 Surprising (a mana-flush: fruit ripens as you watch, a healing herb blooms out of season, wounds knit oddly, a torch burns blue — //opportunity with a cost//) · 6 Error (the Vale exhales: spore-drift, a ponor gasps open, wildlife behaves as one animal, local Arcane +1 for a scene, roll on the region's Layer Bleed).
Each result yields: ''texture'' (3–5 sensory lines) · ''movement modifier'' · ''entanglement tag'' (who noticed — patrol, pilgrim, grove-fae, tally-drover, something below).

!! 10) Weird Handles / Glitch Inserts

* ''The statuary law:'' the empire's stone-and-bronze watchmen never got word it fell (iron ones seized and rotted; stone endured). Default order after five centuries: *an intruder is an intruder.* Some accept ''re-addressing'' — right password, right authority, right offering — over destruction. The watchmen cannot be dismissed; nothing says they cannot be *re-hired.*
* ''The globes are Power Cores.'' The teleport-system globes (45H, 112) read, in Nexus terms, as small pre-Fall Power Cores — fragile, irreplaceable, load-bearing, with a maintenance log nobody living can follow. The Lindwyrm's hunt has targets inside the hill; the campaign decides who realizes it first.
* ''The flushed tide:'' each Drinking, the underground river beaches debris on Level 2 — including items *fresh from centuries ago* (the maelstrom's causal wrack found its way into the mountain's own hydrology). Referee: a small "what the river brought" table pays out every season-turn.
* ''The high-mana texture:'' wild-magic exposure in the Vale skews *floral* — the Mild/Weird table blooms (astral plants, spore-halos, sweet fizz); Severe stays severe. The Vale is generous right up until it is not.
* ''The noon-shaft (F):'' at high noon the surface sun and the garden's false-sun stand in alignment nobody designed. Twice a year the beam reaches the altar ledge *charged*. The cult knows the dates. Now so does one guide.
* ''The Great Gods tie (referee truth):'' the seed-city's palace-temple served the same mystery-cult the storm-watchers kept at Samothraki — the Great Gods, the initiates' grace of *safety at sea and threshold*. The Lindwyrm's monitoring station and the drowned capital's founding sanctuary stand in one communion, 489 years apart — which means somewhere in the sacral heartwood below is an altar that would recognize Telarus' station-signs on sight. Nobody in the Vale knows this. One thing below might.

!! 11) Open Dials (named, not yet rolled)

* Throne menace dial: liberation-dynasty sympathy vs throne-room dread — current draft leans sympathetic-with-teeth; referee may re-arm.
* The langar-scar (the founders' common table): keep / soften / cut.
* G'ruk's fuse: player-lit (current) vs referee-triggered.
* Village table: single d6 (current) vs d6 × d6 village-plus-tension.
* The unnamed builder of the deep cavern: hold open (current) / bind to the reptile stratum / bind to the ley-node / bind to something the Pale Emperor knows.
* Lake-State cadence: season-turn (current) vs per-delve.
* River distance: exact week-leg count Ainos→Vale (5–7 current) and which legs are node-crawl vs hexcrawl — settle when the flashback-arc plays.
* The Below's founding theology: *the day we rose, the sky fell.* Five centuries of the kingdom explaining that coincidence to itself — blessing (the gods broke the world to free us) / curse (our rising cracked something) / silence (the court forbids the question). Which reading rules the Throne, which the dissidents, which the Boiler People whisper? (Shapes everything about how the Below meets the reopened world — and it is the *in-world mirror* of the sealed Death-of-Thanatos question.)
* Deep-strata spacing (~3500–4400): the tomb/temple/golden-age intervals hold as honest approximations; tighten only if play reaches them.
* Drowned-city salvage law: who owns what the Drinking reveals — village custom vs cult claim vs tally-ledger — one dispute-table wanted.

